<Site title="Methodology"><PageHeader title="How we know" eyebrow="Methods & limits" description="The rules behind each number, comparison and sentence on this site." /><div class="shell page-grid"><article><h2 id="source">1. Source chain</h2><p>A public claim must connect to a dated, identifiable source and exact locator. We distinguish the source's observation period, its publication date, our retrieval date and editorial review date. Rebuilding this site does not update a source's observation.</p><p>Several reports repeating a single release are treated as one evidence chain. Corrections and revised inputs can invalidate downstream claims.</p><h2 id="labels">2. Evidence labels</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Observed</th><td>Directly measured by a named observing network or agency.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Reported</th><td>An institution or person says an action or outcome occurred; independent confirmation may be absent.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Derived</th><td>Calculated from listed inputs and a reproducible method.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Forecast</th><td>An issued outlook with a stated validity window and expiry.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Scenario</th><td>Conditional illustration, not an observed or forecast outcome.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Unknown</th><td>Evidence is missing, inaccessible or incompatible.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="comparisons">3. Comparison guard</h2><p>We compare only matching geography or a documented crosswalk, source series, unit, normal period, season cutoff and measurement definition. Reservoir comparisons require a stable monitored group and capacity denominator. Population counts and projections retain their separate vintages and styles.</p><p>A rainfall departure cannot by itself establish drought declaration, usable water, crop loss, household shortage or health effects. Historical analogues also require matched season and source methods; documentary accounts sit apart from instrumental series.</p><h2 id="geography">4. Geography and maps</h2><p>Administrative places, weather subdivisions, catchments, utility service areas and aquifers are distinct. District codes and boundaries change. No district value is filled by distributing a state value. A map appears only when its geometry version, rights and joins have been reviewed; the table remains the primary access path.</p><h2 id="missing">5. Missing and stale data</h2><p>Missing is never zero. A source that could not be retrieved is recorded as a failed attempt, while the last approved observation retains its original date. We do not infer safe conditions from a blank cell.</p><h2 id="rights">6. Reuse and review</h2><p>Only permitted normalized outputs belong in public downloads. Automated checks can validate formats and calculations; they do not replace independent source reading or human review of sensitive reporting. Public release status is tracked separately from software readiness.</p><p><a href={url('/journalists/')}>Downloads and reporting notes ↗</a></p></article><aside><p class="eyebrow">On this page</p><a href="#source">Source chain</a><a href="#labels">Evidence labels</a><a href="#comparisons">Comparison guard</a><a href="#geography">Geography and maps</a><a href="#missing">Missing data</a><a href="#rights">Reuse and review</a></aside></div></Site>
